Treat native timer wakes as advisory samples of a monotonic clock. Compute each
one-shot delay from `origin + ceil(tick_ordinal * 1e9 / tick_hz)` so early,
late, and spurious wakes cannot shift the simulation timeline or accumulate
callback work as drift.

Stamp input in that same clock domain when it arrives. Deliver timestamps at
or before a boundary before its tick, never apply later input retroactively,
and batch only adjacent event-free tick runs. Under overload, drop the oldest
whole tick boundaries while retaining every queued event and deliver events
from dropped time before the first surviving tick. Count dropped ticks visibly.

When hot reload changes the guest-declared rate, replace the scheduler at the
swap instant and generation-cancel the obsolete timer loop; otherwise an old
armed wake can continue driving the new guest's rate.
